<div class="article-body">

<p>I impulse bought an AeroGarden Harvest for $89. It sat on my counter for 8 weeks growing decent basil. I was happy. Then I built a $20 Kratky jar setup from a mason jar, some clay pebbles, and seeds I already had. Same kitchen, same light, same time period.</p>

<p>Here is the short version: <strong>the $20 jar outgrew the $89 AeroGarden.</strong> Not by a little. By a lot. The basil in the jar was taller, thicker, and I harvested about 2.5 times as much. I did not expect that. I thought the expensive kit would win. It did not.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I am not a scientist. I am someone who spent too much money on both options so you do not have to. If you are deciding between these two, here is the exact breakdown of what happened, what each setup costs, and which one you should pick based on your situation.</p>

<h2 id="problem">1. Why I Bought Both</h2>

<p>I wanted fresh herbs year-round. Every beginner guide I read said "get an AeroGarden." It is the obvious pick. Amazon reviews, influencer posts, even my neighbor has one. So I bought the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=AeroGarden+Harvest&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">AeroGarden Harvest</a>. $89. Plug and play. Lights built in, pump built in, timer built in. Fill the tank, drop in the pods, press start.</p>

<p>Then I started reading about Kratky method. No pump. No electricity. A jar, net cup, clay pebbles, nutrients. Total cost for a full Kratky setup: under $20. That number bothered me. $89 vs $20. Both grow plants. One is 4x cheaper. I had to test it.</p>

<p>So I set up both side by side. Same counter, same window light, same seeds (basil from the AeroGarden pods and basil I sprouted myself). AeroGarden on the left, mason jar on the right. Let them run for 3 months and measured everything.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Numbers</div>
  <p>AeroGarden Harvest: $89 + $15/month for replacement pods. DIY Kratky jar: $20 total. DIY setup grew 2.5x more basil in 3 months. The jar cost 78% less and outperformed the kit.</p>
</div>

